[63/1873] GWAM
# sentinel:claim-language-allow
TITLE: GWAM: Ghost-Weighted Aggregate Meta-Analysis for Registry-Based Publication Bias Sensitivity
TYPE: methods  |  ESTIMAND: Integrity ratio (lambda)
DATA: ClinicalTrials.gov registry + 398 Cochrane reviews (Pairwise70)
PATH: C:\Models\GWAM
OJS: https://synthesis-medicine.org/index.php/gnosis/article/view/gwam-ghost-weighted-meta-analysis
      Gnosis Vol. 2 No. 1 | Jun 16 2026

CURRENT BODY (156 words):
Can trial registry data provide a quantitative sensitivity framework for publication bias in meta-analysis? GWAM classifies ClinicalTrials.gov records as published, results-posted, or ghost protocols across 7,467 meta-analyses from 398 Cochrane reviews, computing enrollment-weighted integrity ratios for each drug-condition pair. The method scales published pooled estimates by the integrity ratio lambda, with simulation and hierarchical variance-propagation extensions quantifying uncertainty about unobserved effects. For escitalopram in depression, the adjusted OR on the log scale fell to 0.025 (95% SI âˆ’0.041 to 0.087) versus the published random-effects estimate of 0.255, with lambda of 0.096. Simulation at 2,000 replications confirmed minimal bias at the null and near-nominal hierarchical coverage, while across the Pairwise70 benchmark 7.9 percent of meta-analyses with effects exceeding 0.10 were attenuated below that threshold. GWAM provides transparent registry-anchored sensitivity analysis that complements existing statistical bias corrections. A limitation is that incomplete ClinicalTrials.gov publication linkage inflates ghost classification, making lambda a lower bound on true publication fraction.

